## Problem

`RunState::hasVisited()` (`src/Runtime/RunState.php:46-49`) does a strict `in_array` scan over the full path on every call. It is invoked once per transition by the acyclic revisit guard in `GuideRunner` (`src/Runtime/GuideRunner.php:135`), so acyclic runs are O(N²) in path length. #20 indexed `GuideDefinition::edgesFrom()` by origin node but left the matching path scan behind — the edges got an index, the path didn't.

## Proposed solution

Derive a set once in the constructor and make `hasVisited()` a lookup. The set is a pure function of `path`, so it never needs to be stored, copied, or serialized separately:

```php
final readonly class RunState
{
    /** @var array<string, true> */
    private array $visited;

    public function __construct(
        // ... existing promoted properties unchanged ...
    ) {
        $this->visited = array_fill_keys($this->path, true);
    }

    public function hasVisited(string $nodeKey): bool
    {
        return isset($this->visited[$nodeKey]);
    }
}
```

Notes:
- A non-promoted property initialized in the constructor body is legal in a `readonly` class, so `final readonly` is preserved.
- `moveTo()`/`copy()` pass `path` through the constructor, so the set is always derived from the authoritative path — no dual bookkeeping.
- `toArray()`/`fromArray()` are unchanged: hydration re-derives the set from the stored path, keeping `RunState` backward-hydratable (the #22 constraint).
- Public API and behaviour are unchanged; path order and duplicate-key semantics (cyclic profiles re-visit legitimately) are untouched — `hasVisited` remains a membership test, not an ordering one.

## SemVer

**Fix (patch) bump.** Internal performance improvement with zero public API or behaviour change — same shape as #20.
